  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 304 AM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nThe main forecast change with this package is that the storm system \nthat looked to produce light accumulating snow across the eastern \nCWA Wednesday night into Thanksgiving, has been trending more \nprogressive with better chances for accumulating snow now forecast \nto remain east of the CWA. However, out ahead of this system today, \ncool and cloudy conditions are forecast. Early this morning, a \novercast cirrus shield is streaming north into the area out ahead of \na upper level trough that will move through the area today. At a \nsurface, a ridge of high pressure is dominating the area which will \nprovide for weak mixing today. That cloud cover and weak mixing will \nhold highs in the upper 30s to lower 40s today. Tonight into \nWednesday, the upper level system that was looking to be the \npotential snowmaker, will dig south out of the northern Plains as a \nclosed upper level low. This upper level low is forecast to move \ndirectly over he forecast Wednesday. Some model try to produce a few \nlight snow showers or flurries across northwestern Missouri and \nnortheastern Kansas however, the sub-cloud layer below 7-8kft looks \ndry enough to preclude any precipitation. A weak surface ridge will \nremain in control over the forecast area Wednesday again providing \nweak mixing under cloudy skies hold highs in the in the upper 30s to \nmid 40s.\n\nWednesday night into Thanksgiving morning...the closed upper level \nlow will move into the mid-Mississippi River Valley. Models than \ndiffer on how quickly to shift this feature further east into the \nOhio River Valley. This will be the difference between whether or \nnot the eastern CWA sees snow on Thanksgiving. The EC is the slowest \nof all the solutions and does bring snow to areas along and east of \nthe Highway 63 corridor. However, as it is producing snow, surface \ntemperatures remain above freezing with warm ground temperatures. \nConsequently, rates would have to be heavy enough to produce \naccumulations. That being said, even if this slower solution did \ncome to fruition, light accumulation less than an inch would be \npossible. The GFS and RRFS provide solutions that are more \nprogressive moving the upper level low eastward through the Ohio \nRiver valley on Thanksgiving which would keep the local area dry. \nHighs Thanksgiving are forecast to rise into the low to mid 40s. \nFriday, as the system moves to the east the upper level flow becomes \nquasi-zonal across the region allowing highs to rise into the upper \n40s to mid 50s. Friday night, a upper level shortwave will move \nthrough the Upper Midwest force a weak cold front through the area. \nModest westerly flow behind this front on Saturday will provide \ndeeper mixing pushing highs into the 50s. Sunday, zonal flow aloft \ncouple with weak WAA will keep highs in the 50s. Monday a surface \nthrough is forecast to bisect the area as a upper level trough moves \ninto the central Plains. This will produce a few light showers with \nhighs again in the 50s.",
